# /etc/steady-walker.env: device names and pins for Sophi's walker service. The ALSA names and
# WALKER_GPIOMON were checked on our Pi 5 on 2026-10-24; the GPIO pins below are still examples.
# The ALSA names are for our parts: the SF-558 USB gooseneck mic (the kernel names its card SF558) and our
# USB soundbar (named Speaker by systemd/85-walker-audio.rules, which the installer puts in place). For any
# other mic or speaker, find the names with `arecord -L` and `aplay -L` (devices/pi/README.md, step 5).
# Prefer CARD= names, which survive replugging, over card numbers.
WALKER_ALSA_CAPTURE=plughw:CARD=SF558,DEV=0
# A `default:CARD=...` playback name goes through ALSA's mixer, so a cue sound can play over speech.
WALKER_ALSA_PLAYBACK=default:CARD=Speaker

# Optional Piper voice model (README, "Optional: the Piper voice"). Without it (or if Piper fails) the
# walker speaks with espeak-ng.
#WALKER_PIPER_MODEL=/opt/steady-walker/voices/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx

# GPIO buttons, each wired from the pin to ground (README step 3). Line numbers are BCM GPIO numbers.
# The Pi can't sense a button, so it watches and reports them only when you say they're wired:
#WALKER_GPIO_BUTTONS=wired
WALKER_GPIO_CHIP=gpiochip0
WALKER_GPIO_TALK=17
WALKER_GPIO_CONFIRM=27
# libgpiod's command-line version (`gpiomon --version`): v2 on the current Raspberry Pi OS, trixie (2.2.1,
# checked on the Pi 5 on 2026-10-24); v1 on the older Bookworm (1.6.x).
WALKER_GPIOMON=v2

# Optional USB NMEA GPS receiver. Use its /dev/serial/by-path link after plugging it in; the walker
# serial scanner excludes this port, so it cannot mistake GPS sentences for ESP32 messages. The GPS
# bring-up command reads locally. The running service reads and sends a recent fix only after the
# walker user enables sharing on their phone; leave this unset until the receiver's tty is verified.
#WALKER_GPS_PORT=/dev/serial/by-path/REPLACE_WITH_GPS_PORT
#WALKER_GPS_BAUD=9600

# The walker screen's page on http://127.0.0.1:<port>/ (README, "The walker screen's page"): another port, or
# off. The kiosk's placeholder looks for 8740. A greeting name for the clock screen (up to 12 characters):
#WALKER_SCREEN_PORT=8740
#WALKER_SCREEN_GREETING=Nan
